/*
  Psychiatric Interviewing Trainer — Study guide, Chapter 14: Personality Disorders: How to Sensitively Arrive at a Differential Diagnosis
  Written in our own words as a companion to Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding (3rd ed.).
  Clinical exchanges are composed for teaching; they are not transcripts from the book. Book cases are summarized.
  Example probe and entrance questions are composed; the book's full lists are not reproduced.
  Schema: see guide-ch1.js.
*/
window.TRAINER = window.TRAINER || { parts: {} };
window.TRAINER.guides = window.TRAINER.guides || {};
window.TRAINER.guides.ch14 = {
  chapter: "ch14",
  intro: "Chapter 13 laid out the theory; Chapter 14 is the bedside method. Shea opens with Emily Dickinson, who spoke to visitors from behind a closed door and offered one guest the choice of a glass of wine or a rose, and asks what a modern clinician would need to coax someone like her out from behind the door. His answer has four parts. First, a vivid survey of the ten DSM-5 personality disorders, grouped by how life feels from the inside: anxiety-prone, poorly empathic, and psychotic-prone. Second, the errors that spoil personality diagnosis: label slapping, countertransference, and the opposite mistake of refusing to diagnose at all. Third, a two-step method that fits into the 7 to 12 minutes an intake usually has for personality: narrow the field with signal signs, signal symptoms, and probe questions, then expand the one or two likeliest disorders while checking that each trait is long-standing, independent of any current illness, and severe enough to be pathological. Fourth, dimensional traits, which can reveal hidden strengths as well as problems. The probe-questions drill, the practice items, and the simulation 'Tracks in the snow' are built on this chapter.",

  highYield: {
    mustKnow: [
      "Know the disorders as people, not checklists. Shea groups the ten by how life feels: [[Anxiety-prone, poorly empathic, psychotic-prone|anxiety-prone]] (obsessive–compulsive, dependent, avoidant), poorly empathic (schizoid, antisocial, histrionic, narcissistic), and psychotic-prone (borderline, schizotypal, paranoid, all liable to [[Micropsychotic episode|micropsychotic episodes]]). It is his working map, not the DSM-5's Clusters A, B, and C.",
      "The poorly empathic disorders miss others' needs in different ways: the narcissistic person is oblivious to them, the antisocial person sees them and uses them, the histrionic person is swept past them by the drama of the moment, and the schizoid person has so muted an inner life that others' feelings barely register.",
      "Look-alikes to separate: schizoid (no wish for people, so no fear of rejection) versus avoidant (longs for people, fears humiliation) versus schizotypal (wants contact, is rejection-sensitive, and lives in a vivid, odd inner world on the schizophrenia spectrum). Dependent (runs toward a rescuer) versus avoidant (dares not approach anyone). [[Stable vs unstable narcissism|Stable narcissism]] (truly feels superior) versus unstable narcissism (a grandiose front over a fragile, easily enraged self, prone to depression and micropsychosis).",
      "[[Label slapping]]: diagnosing from the hour's behavior ('so manipulative, clearly borderline'). Behavior in the room and intuition point to regions worth exploring; the diagnosis is historical. A dramatic, flirtatious, mildly pressured first visit may be the start of a first manic episode, cyclothymia, a stimulant, or even a brain tumor.",
      "The labels carry heavy freight ('just another damned sociopath'). A clinician who judges morally will struggle to ask about traits that make patients feel guilty; a judging glance intensifies the guilt, activates the patient's [[Self-system]], and information is distorted or withheld. Gentle, non-judgmental questions and [[Unconditional positive regard]] are what make personality data valid.",
      "The opposite error is refusing to diagnose a personality disorder in one hour. It makes the same mistake as label slapping, judging by presentation. A hypomanic patient can look histrionic for months. The real limits are time to explore the history and the validity of the patient's account. If both are met, the diagnosis can be made at intake.",
      "Behaviorally concrete diagnoses (antisocial, borderline, schizotypal) are often makeable in one interview, provided the patient is truthful and the traits are not [[State dependency|state dependent]]. Subjective ones (histrionic, narcissistic) are harder in the time available; aim for a provisional or rule-out diagnosis and use collateral sources. Spotting borderline personality disorder early matters for triage: not to a trainee who is leaving, and not to a clinician who cannot manage the work.",
      "Step 1, limit the field. No one can explore ten disorders in an hour; find the one, two, or three most likely. Passively, watch for [[Signal sign|signal signs]] (behaviors in the room, often in the first 5 to 8 minutes) and listen for [[Signal symptom|signal symptoms]] (things the patient reports). Actively, ask [[Probe question|probe questions]].",
      "Signal signs: caustic comments about the interview or interviewer ([[Process comment|process comments]]; antisocial, narcissistic, histrionic, borderline), unwarranted complaints and demands (narcissistic, borderline, paranoid, antisocial), sexual innuendo or charm aimed at the interviewer (antisocial, histrionic, narcissistic; lewder means think antisocial), dramatic bravado and tears switched on and off (histrionic, borderline), childlike helplessness (dependent, sometimes histrionic or borderline), and open manipulation such as inviting criticism of another clinician (borderline, histrionic, narcissistic, and passive-aggressive traits).",
      "Signal symptoms: self-mutilation or frequent suicidal behavior (borderline), extreme perfectionism and 'never done enough' (obsessive–compulsive), run-ins with the law (antisocial; ask specifically about disorderly conduct, drunk driving, and speeding, which patients omit), frequent anger and violence (antisocial, borderline, and less often paranoid, narcissistic, histrionic), and profound low self-esteem (dependent, avoidant, schizotypal, borderline). The three group themes (anxiety, poor empathy, micropsychotic breaks) are signal symptoms too.",
      "Probe questions are woven in through natural and referred gates, never fired as a list. One or two answers in the expected direction justify expansion. Probes rule out as efficiently as they rule in: the self-described 'party animal' is not schizoid (unless currently manic, since a state can hide a trait as well as mimic one). The social history is where personality leaps out, and by then the alliance is strong enough for sensitive questions.",
      "Step 2, expand the likeliest disorder, criterion by criterion, conversationally. Three validity checks: historical persistence (anchor repeatedly to adolescence: 'From high school on, has it been typical for you...?', about five times per expansion), not state dependent ('When you're feeling your normal self, not depressed...'; otherwise patients blur time periods), and pathological severity.",
      "Severity: a trait counts only if it is rigid and severe enough to cause the patient or others real problems. Not all actors are histrionic and not all surgeons narcissistic; a good emergency nurse needs more than average attention to detail. [[Tapping for epiphenomena]] checks this fast. After a 'yes', ask a generic epiphenomena question ('What makes you say that?', 'Give me an example'), watching how readily and naturally the example comes. If still unclear, ask a specific one about a typical consequence ('Do people complain you're too slow to finish?').",
      "Stay engaged while expanding: sidetrack when the patient needs to, use humor, and don't take the bait. When a patient with borderline traits announces that she will cut again and you can't stop her, agree calmly that only she can stop it and return to it later; alarm invites a war of nerves. Coolness right after your kindness is defense more than manipulation.",
      "Dimensions: the DSM-5's [[Alternative DSM-5 Model for Personality Disorders|Alternative Model]] rates 25 trait facets in five domains (negative affectivity, detachment, antagonism, disinhibition, psychoticism). Too slow for an intake (Shea estimates an hour done well), but individual traits make excellent entry points and treatment targets. Shea proposes adding traits as optional specifiers to the categorical system.",
      "A trait that is absent can be a strength. Nancy, who has borderline personality disorder, turned out to be the opposite of impulsive, a chronic second-guesser, and her clinician reframed that as a rare strength to harness against her angry outbursts. Not everyone with a diagnosis shows every typical trait, and naming a strength gives a person with chronic worthlessness something to be proud of.",
      "[[Prototype-based system|Prototype-based systems]] such as the SWAP-II diagnose by matching the patient to a narrative portrait rather than counting criteria. Their strength is validity and [[Descriptive essence]]. And in the end, clinicians are professional story collectors; the intensity of rage, dependency, or suicidal angst can tempt us to stop listening before the story is told."
    ],
    pitfalls: [
      "Diagnosing histrionic personality from one colorful, flirtatious, mildly pressured hour, and missing the first manic episode (or the stimulant, or the tumor).",
      "Writing 'borderline' because the patient was manipulative in the interview, with no history of the pattern since adolescence.",
      "Saying you never diagnose personality disorders in a first interview, so the diagnosis is found only after a trainee who is leaving has built a dependency.",
      "Running through all ten disorders, or reading probe questions as a checklist, so the interview becomes hurried and stilted and the patient doesn't return.",
      "Asking about traits with a judging tone or a parental glance, so the patient feels publicly shamed and withholds or distorts.",
      "Accepting 'yes, I'm a perfectionist' as meeting the criterion without tapping for epiphenomena.",
      "Letting the patient blur periods of depression or mania with their usual self, so state-dependent behavior is counted as a trait.",
      "Asking about arrests and missing the drunk-driving and disorderly-conduct charges patients leave out.",
      "Concluding a patient isn't schizoid because he's a 'party animal' this week, when he is manic.",
      "Reacting with visible alarm when a patient challenges you over self-harm, and being drawn into a war of nerves.",
      "Feeling 'jerked around' and responding with irritation, instead of recognizing a defense against pain.",
      "Exploring traits only for pathology, and missing the absent trait that is actually a strength."
    ],
    phrases: [
      { say: "You've changed jobs often. Did any of those endings have to do with trouble with the law, or a boss who made you leave? Sometimes that's why people move on quickly.", when: "Probing the antisocial region from a signal symptom, with shame attenuation. Composed." },
      { say: "If you had a bit more skill with people, would you want to be around them more, or do you honestly prefer your own company?", when: "A probe that separates avoidant (wants people, fears hurt) from schizoid (doesn't want them). Composed." },
      { say: "Thinking back to high school, was that true of you then too, or is it more recent?", when: "Anchoring a trait to adolescence; use it several times in each expansion. Composed." },
      { say: "Leaving aside the recent weeks when you've been depressed, when you're your usual self, how often do you end up breaking things when you're angry?", when: "Ruling out state dependency by naming the time period explicitly. Composed." },
      { say: "What makes you say you're a perfectionist? Give me an example.", when: "A generic epiphenomena question; watch how readily and naturally the example comes. Composed." },
      { say: "Do you ever get to laugh freely, or is there always something you feel you should be doing?", when: "A specific epiphenomena question for an overly serious devotion to work. Composed." },
      { say: "You may be right that I can't stop you; in the end only you can. Let's put that aside for now and come back to it.", when: "Declining a challenge over self-harm without alarm or a power struggle. Composed." },
      { say: "When a friend leaves and the door closes behind them, what happens to your mood?", when: "Exploring intolerance of being alone and emptiness in the borderline region, through a concrete moment. Composed." },
      { say: "Most people I've met with your diagnosis struggle with acting on impulse. You don't. That's a real strength, and I think we can use it.", when: "Naming a hidden strength found while exploring a dimensional trait. Composed." }
    ]
  },

  sections: [
    {
      id: "anxiety-prone",
      title: "The survey, part 1: the anxiety-prone disorders",
      lede: "Why a survey of portraits, Shea's three groups, and the obsessive–compulsive, dependent, and avoidant personalities.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Diagnosis means expanding the actual DSM-5 criteria, so you must know them; but the criteria sound sterile, and the first step toward interviewing well is a feel for how each disorder looks in a living person. Shea therefore offers vivid portraits, drawing on the clinical literature, his own experience, and the narrative style of the Shedler–Westen Assessment Procedure (SWAP-II). He warns that they are characterizations: some people present this intensely, many less so, and real people vary widely. Use them to recognize patterns and recall criteria in the moment, never as stereotypes, and read the DSM-5 criteria alongside them." },
        { type: "p", text: "He groups the ten disorders by their core experience of life, so that recognizing a pervasive worldview during the interview immediately suggests a cluster to explore: [[Anxiety-prone, poorly empathic, psychotic-prone|anxiety-prone, poorly empathic, and psychotic-prone]] (the last meaning a tendency toward micropsychotic episodes, Chapter 11). The DSM-5 uses a similar but different scheme: odd–eccentric (Cluster A), dramatic–emotional (B), anxious–fearful (C). Any clinician may organize the disorders in whatever way works; this is Shea's." },
        { type: "p", text: "The anxiety-prone disorders share a life riddled with tension and anxiety, differing in how the anxiety shows and how it is handled. Others feel anxiety too, but here it is the keynote. All three are vulnerable to bouts of depression when needs go unmet or defenses fail." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Disorder", "How life feels", "How the anxiety is handled", "Telltale details"], rows: [
          ["Obsessive–compulsive", "A pressure cooker of the person's own perfectionistic demands; any failure is total; a covert belief that love must be earned", "Control: intellectualizing, discipline, lists, schedules; fear of losing control", "Workaholic who loves and resents work; serious, cool exterior; a 'schedule for play'; agonizing over decisions, life a corridor of one-way doors; tears felt but not shown"],
          ["Dependent", "The world is full of potential disaster", "Runs toward a rescuer, a surrogate parent; the 'white knight' is the invited dinner guest", "Warm, giving, nearly obsequious; will do almost anything, even objectionable or illegal things, to be accepted; anger shown passively (mistakes, procrastination, 'forgetting'); avoids decisions; may be unable to leave an abusive partner"],
          ["Avoidant", "Desperate for affection but certain of rejection; self-esteem laced with brutal self-ridicule", "Avoids people altogether; socially phobic; cool and aloof as armor", "Doesn't dare approach a rescuer; testing comments ('you probably don't want me along, but...') that exhaust others; may be targeted by bullies; years in a city without a friend; nights online or with the television, companions who cannot hurt"]
        ] },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Passive-aggressive traits", text: "Shea also keeps the [[Passive-aggressive personality]] in view, from the DSM-IV-TR appendix rather than the DSM-5: anxiety muted by low expectations ('I never expected much from this lousy show'), resentment of being taken for granted, and opposition expressed through slowness, half-effort, and pointing out the flaws in others' plans." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "drill", id: "personality-probes", label: "Drill: Personality probe questions" }]
    },

    {
      id: "poorly-empathic",
      title: "The survey, part 2: the poorly empathic disorders",
      lede: "Schizoid, antisocial, histrionic, and narcissistic personalities, and four different ways of missing other people's feelings.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "These four share an unusual inability to empathize as most people do, or as reliably. Their histories may be littered with people who felt used or betrayed, or, with the schizoid person, simply with an absence of interest in others. In the interview you may glimpse a world in which other people's feelings count for little. Following Westen and Shedler, the self-centeredness takes four different forms." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Schizoid and antisocial", items: [
            "Schizoid: the quiet loner, a shell-dwelling creature content to live as an isolated unit; a bland inner and outer world; emotions neither high nor deep; tenderness neither felt nor sought; indifferent to others' opinions. The colorlessness is a muted palette, not superiority. No fear of rejection because there is no wish for acceptance. Not on the schizophrenia spectrum and not prone to micropsychosis.",
            "Antisocial: a chameleon, belligerent with one person and charming with the next, because others are pieces in a game. Lying, cheating, job hopping, trouble with the law, cruelty online behind false identities, sex without responsibility; at worst sadistic and violent (sociopathy). Little anxiety, little guilt; problems blamed on others; an uncanny knack for getting yet another chance ('this time is different'; it isn't).",
            "Vaillant's humanizing point: they probably did feel pain, and callousness may defend against being engulfed by dependency needs. They may be as trapped as their victims: all too human."
          ] },
          b: { title: "Histrionic and narcissistic", items: [
            "Histrionic: exhilarating on a good day, miserable on a bad one; an adult hoping for a perch on a parent's knee; the world seen impressionistically, details blurred and forgotten; little sense of responsibility (the eviction is days away, but there is a date to land); flirtatious, sometimes promiscuous, drawn to people already taken; life lived as a movie, demanding center stage; beneath it a fragile self-esteem dependent on applause. Mild suicidal behavior without intent to die, followed by a bright smile when 'Mr. or Mrs. Right' appears: mood driven by circumstance, easily distracted like a child's tantrum.",
            "Narcissistic, stable variant: truly feels superior and enjoys their own company; others exist to comfort them; flips from idealizing to denigrating like a child about a toy; often a spoiled upbringing; grandiose fantasies cover being ordinary; can be reasonably happy if adulation keeps coming, and pouts, rages, gets depressed, or turns to substances if it stops.",
            "Narcissistic, unstable variant: a poorly developed self under constant threat of annihilation and worthlessness, defended by grandiosity; easily wounded, turns on friends in a moment, bitter, gloats over others' embarrassment, throws dinner on the floor when it's late; impossible to please; prone to severe depression and micropsychotic episodes; sometimes controlled in public and primitive with spouse or therapist. Pretends to be Napoleon and knows, deep down, it is a sham."
          ] }
        },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Four ways to miss a feeling", text: "The narcissistic person is oblivious to others' needs. The antisocial person sees them clearly and uses them. The histrionic person misses them, swept away by the drama of the moment. The schizoid person lacks so much inner emotional life that they cannot recognize feelings in others. Knowing which kind of empathic failure you are seeing narrows the differential quickly." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c14p2", label: "Practice: using a probe to rule out" }]
    },

    {
      id: "psychotic-prone",
      title: "The survey, part 3: the psychotic-prone disorders",
      lede: "Borderline, schizotypal, and paranoid personalities, with childlike defenses and a tendency toward micropsychotic breaks.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "In these three the sense of self, the ego structure and its spontaneous defenses, is seriously delayed developmentally; the defenses resemble a child's: magical thinking, preoccupation with fantasy worlds, impulsive or rage-driven action. Under pressure the defenses can sweep the person into a false reality, a [[Micropsychotic episode]], hence 'psychotic-prone' (Shea also calls them the regressed personality disorders). The histrionic and unstable narcissistic personalities can function as regressed personalities at moments of extreme stress, with micropsychotic episodes of their own." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Disorder", "Inner experience", "What you see", "Micropsychosis and course"], rows: [
          ["Borderline", "No inner self; hollow like clothing hanging in a closet, empty unless filled by others; being alone can bring a sense of annihilation; a friend leaving feels like losing a part of oneself", "Hostile dependency; rages when slighted (glasses thrown, furniture broken); exquisite sensitivity to hurt that exhausts friends; stimulation sought through drugs, sex, eating; black-or-white thinking, catastrophizing, inability to self-soothe; recurrent suicidal thoughts; self-cutting, burning, head banging, often painless and relieving, after an argument or slight", "Painless cutting probably reflects fleeting psychotic depersonalization; derealization and paranoia also occur. 'The glass people': delicate, easily broken, dangerous when shattered"],
          ["Schizotypal", "Also coreless and empty, with the emptiness filled by vivid fantasy: clairvoyant messages, ghostly presences, magical hunches, secretive glances", "Wants contact but doesn't know how; rejection-sensitive; withdraws to books, online worlds, or (one of Shea's adolescent patients) running with stray dogs, where he was king rather than 'the dog boy'; idiosyncratic, metaphoric, vague speech; eccentric gestures, perhaps like Dickinson's wine-or-rose", "Decompensates under stress into delusions and hallucinations; on the schizophrenia spectrum, with greater risk of schizophrenia or affected relatives. Lives 'inside the bottle'"],
          ["Paranoid", "The world as a hostile place; trust never developed; restless worrying; a deep sense of inferiority and fear that weaknesses invite attack", "Suspicious, guarded, scouring every exchange for deception, fixing on a slip of the tongue; over-controlling, intensely jealous; haughty, unable to admit mistakes; every new face a potential enemy (one patient began writing down the interview 'so people outside know what's going on in here')", "Ideas not delusional except in micropsychotic episodes, which bring rage and indignation; projection attributes their own hostility to others; isolation removes correction; predisposed to paranoid delusional disorder; exquisitely unhappy"]
        ] },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Schizoid is not schizotypal", text: "The two share little but spelling. Both may have few friends and seem aloof, but the schizoid person is bland, has no wish for contact, and is unrelated to schizophrenia; the schizotypal person is rejection-sensitive (more like the avoidant person), has an intensely active and odd inner world 'like a dream on feet', and sits on the schizophrenia spectrum, prone to micropsychotic episodes." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch14", label: "Quiz: Chapter 14" }]
    },

    {
      id: "errors",
      title: "Three errors: label slapping, countertransference, and refusing to diagnose",
      lede: "How clinicians misuse the system, and why the real limits on a one-hour diagnosis are time and validity.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "These problems come less from weaknesses in the diagnostic system than from how clinicians use it. The first is diagnosing too quickly without checking the criteria, the impressionistic diagnosis: 'she's clearly borderline, she was so manipulative in there.' Behavior in the interview and intuition are useful, but their use is to point toward regions worth exploring, now or in a later session. The diagnosis is historical; the hour gives suggestive, not conclusive, evidence. This is [[Label slapping]]." },
        { type: "case", title: "The dramatic first visit", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A patient arrives in a seductive blouse and bright pants, speaks with mild pressure, tells colorful tales, acts coyly, and is caught in several small lies. An inexperienced clinician writes histrionic personality disorder. A careful one learns that this behavior is atypical for her, neither consistent nor persistent, that she has a history of depressions and a family history of bipolar disorder: this is the beginning of her first manic episode, and she may need lithium or another mood stabilizer. The impressionistic diagnostician may not realize it until she is in a frank manic crisis. Atypical bipolar disorder, cyclothymia, amphetamine use, and organic causes such as a brain tumor can produce the same picture, and a premature personality label could delay treatment of any of them." },
        { type: "p", text: "Countertransference is the second. These labels carry negative and sometimes pejorative connotations; a clinician who quickly dislikes an abusive patient may mentally file him as 'just another sociopath', and few of us are above it, so explore what these diagnoses mean to you personally. They affect the patient's future care (Shea has seen clinics refuse a patient because 'we don't have room for any more borderlines'). More deeply, a clinician prone to moral judgment will struggle to interview people with these character structures. Many of the traits you must ask about arouse guilt, a judgmental attitude intensifies it until the interview feels like public humiliation, and a parental glance can punish like a scarlet letter. The more the patient's [[Self-system]] is activated, the more information is withheld or distorted. Skill at uncovering personality pathology closely parallels skill at asking about sensitive matters in an unassuming, natural, non-judgmental way, with the [[Unconditional positive regard]] of Chapter 2." },
        { type: "p", text: "The third error is the reverse: becoming almost phobic about making a personality diagnosis in an hour ('it takes many sessions to see if they present consistently'). Ironically it repeats the label slapper's mistake of judging by presentation; a hypomanic patient can look histrionic for months. The question is not whether the patient behaves histrionically for seven sessions but whether they have done so consistently since adolescence. Two things limit a first-interview diagnosis: enough time to explore the history for consistency and persistence, and reasonably valid information. When both are present, the diagnosis can safely be made." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Often diagnosable in one interview", items: [
            "Antisocial, borderline, schizotypal",
            "Criteria are concrete and behavioral: either the patient was suspended from school or not; either there is a pattern of self-mutilation or not",
            "Requires that the patient is truthful and the traits are not [[State dependency|state dependent]] (caused by mania, depression, psychosis, substances, epilepsy, a tumor)"
          ] },
          b: { title: "Harder in 7 to 12 minutes", items: [
            "Histrionic, narcissistic",
            "Criteria are largely subjective and need many historical situations to confirm",
            "Aim for a provisional diagnosis or a set of rule-outs; family and other sources are especially valuable"
          ] }
        },
        { type: "p", text: "Excessive caution has costs. It breeds sloppiness ('if you can't make the diagnosis, why try?'), becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy as the skill goes unpracticed, and above all fails the patient. An intake interviewer who recognizes borderline personality disorder can steer the patient away from an inexperienced clinician, or one with serious problems of their own; a therapist can think twice before changing session frequency for a patient with borderline or severe dependent personality disorder; and, as Chapter 13 showed, a clinician leaving in six months can refer to someone who is staying." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c14p5", label: "Practice: avoiding label slapping" }]
    },

    {
      id: "limit-field",
      title: "Step 1: limiting the field with signal signs and symptoms",
      lede: "What to notice without asking anything, and Ms. Dole's microphone.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The method has two steps. First narrow the ten possibilities to the one, two, or perhaps three most likely; no one can sensitively cover all ten in fifty minutes, and trying produces a hurried, stilted interview and a no-show. Then explore those diagnoses thoroughly. Narrowing begins passively: throughout the interview you reflect on the patient's observable behavior and style ([[Signal sign|signal signs]]) and on what they report ([[Signal symptom|signal symptoms]]). Neither proves a disorder or even a criterion; they suggest where to look historically. Shea is struck by how regularly signal signs appear in the first 5 to 8 minutes, the scouting period (the 'A', assessment, of [[PACE]] in Chapter 3), probably because the anxiety of meeting a clinician calls up the person's most ingrained defenses. They may appear later too. His list is drawn from experience, not research, and every clinician will build their own." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Signal sign", "Example", "Regions to consider"], rows: [
          ["Comments on the interview or interviewer (process comments), often caustic", "A self-described 'sensitive' young man tells a struggling student interviewer, in front of classmates, that the student seems to be having more trouble with the interview than he is", "Antisocial, narcissistic, histrionic, borderline; passive-aggressive traits"],
          ["Unwarranted complaints and demands", "Announcing at the door that they won't wait that long next time; a smiling remark that the clinic really should fix its parking", "Narcissistic, borderline, paranoid, antisocial"],
          ["Too pleased with the clinician: sexual innuendo, requests for a phone number, charm about mutual attractiveness", "Overly frank sexual stories; lewder or intrusive innuendo, sometimes used by men with antisocial traits to put female interviewers on guard and control the interview", "Antisocial, histrionic, narcissistic (lewder: think antisocial)"],
          ["Dramatic bravado", "Bold colors, a sinewy scarf, gym-tight clothes or provocative dress; animated gestures; tears that come fast and stop faster when the subject changes", "Histrionic, borderline"],
          ["Childlike helplessness", "Quiet meekness, eagerness to please: 'Is this what you wanted to hear?'", "Dependent; episodically histrionic or borderline"],
          ["Open manipulation", "Inviting you to criticize their therapist ('Don't you think that's strange for a therapist?'); bargaining for control ('if we must talk, let's be quick, and I need a cigarette first'); sighing compliance", "Borderline, histrionic, narcissistic; passive-aggressive traits"]
        ] },
        { type: "case", title: "Ms. Dole", text: "Summarized from the chapter (she also appears in Chapter 8). A lanky woman in worn corduroys and a faded blouse, long dull hair, came in 'having problems coping.' While both she and the interviewer fitted their microphones for a taped interview, she fumbled with hers until the interviewer noticed; just before that, she had actually managed to attach it and then pulled it off. Asked if she could manage, she looked up with pleading eyes and silently shook her head, like a three-year-old asking for help, and the interviewer attached it for her (a parent–child nonverbal reciprocal). Then she handed over notes she had written days earlier and spent about a minute fumbling for the passage she wanted read. Helpless ineffectuality plus manipulative staging had quickly taken control of the interview, pointing to borderline, dependent, and histrionic regions. Detailed interviewing later showed a mixed personality disorder with histrionic and dependent traits. Sometimes the interviewer's eyes are more useful than questions." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Self-mutilation or frequent suicidal behavior (cutting, burning, head banging, repeated overdoses): borderline.",
          "Extreme perfectionism with a nagging sense of never having done enough, a superego run amok: obsessive–compulsive.",
          "Run-ins with the law (arrests, burglary, fights, fencing drugs, prostitution): antisocial. Ask specifically about disorderly conduct, drunk driving, and speeding, which patients tend to leave out.",
          "Frequent anger, sometimes violence: a fan-shaped differential, antisocial or borderline most likely, then paranoid, narcissistic, histrionic.",
          "Extreme low self-esteem: dependent, avoidant, schizotypal, borderline.",
          "The group themes work as signal symptoms too: persistent anxiety (obsessive–compulsive, dependent, avoidant), poor empathy (antisocial, histrionic, schizoid, narcissistic), frequent micropsychotic breaks (borderline, schizotypal, paranoid).",
          "None of this costs extra time; you notice it while taking the presenting problem, history, and other symptoms. With experience it becomes second nature, and in 30 minutes many patients will practically point the way."
        ] }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "sim", id: "sim-tracks", label: "Simulation: Tracks in the snow" }]
    },

    {
      id: "probes",
      title: "Step 1, continued: probe questions",
      lede: "Actively testing a region, ruling in and ruling out, and where in the interview to do it.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The active way to narrow the field is the [[Probe question]], a brief question testing whether a disorder's region deserves expansion. Shea provides about four for each disorder, several developed by Jeremy Roberts, with the answer that points toward the disorder; read the full list in the book. One or two answers in the expected direction usually justify pursuing the disorder; several make it much more likely. Some probes overlap (jealousy, for instance, also turns up in narcissistic, histrionic, and borderline personalities), but each list was chosen for relative specificity. The table summarizes what the probes target, with composed examples." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Region", "What the probes test", "A composed example"], rows: [
          ["Obsessive–compulsive", "Driving oneself hard, perfectionism, lists and checking, not being seen as light-hearted", "Do you usually feel you should be doing just a little more than you already are?"],
          ["Passive-aggressive (DSM-IV-TR appendix)", "Feeling taken for granted, being nagged, half-effort as protest, deflating others' optimism", "When a boss gives you a task you think is pointless, do you sometimes let it go slowly to make your point?"],
          ["Dependent", "Fear of arguing lest the partner stop liking you, planning the day around the partner, preferring others to decide, dreams of being cared for", "In your relationship, who usually makes the big decisions, and how would you prefer it?"],
          ["Avoidant", "Lifelong worry about not being liked, feeling inadequate, very few friends, wariness after being hurt", "Have you mostly kept a very small circle, because opening up to people has hurt before?"],
          ["Schizoid", "Preferring solitude, indifference to others' opinions, not seeing oneself as emotional, one or two friends in a lifetime", "Do you enjoy being around people, or do you much prefer being on your own?"],
          ["Antisocial", "Ease of lying when it seems warranted, arrests or stops for unruly behavior, drugs, or drunk driving, handling oneself in fights, resenting orders", "If you felt a situation called for it, would lying come easily to you?"],
          ["Histrionic", "Being found attractive (answered without embarrassment), ending up the center of attention, intense emotion, thinking one would make a good actor", "Do you find you often end up the center of attention, even when you don't mean to?"],
          ["Narcissistic", "Others not up to one's standards, putting people in their place, quick anger at criticism, being special (answered with assurance)", "When you're honest about it, do most people fall a bit short of your standards?"],
          ["Borderline", "Frequent feelings of being let down, urges to self-harm when hurt, anger at others several times a week, being seen as moody", "When someone close hurts you, do you ever feel like hurting yourself?"],
          ["Schizotypal", "Unusual thoughts others don't have, a sense of being watched, special powers or magical influence, expecting rejection for being odd", "Have you ever felt you had some unusual ability, like ESP or an influence over others?"],
          ["Paranoid", "Expecting disloyalty, easy jealousy, keeping information close, being taken advantage of", "Do you find people tend to be disloyal or dishonest, so it pays to keep things to yourself?"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Probes are never a checklist. Weave them in with graceful transitions, natural and referred gates (Chapter 4), and expect to use only a few per region. Probes eliminate as efficiently as they confirm: to 'do you really enjoy being around people or prefer being alone?', the answer 'I love people, I'm a party animal' tells you a schizoid personality is not in the room (unless, Shea jokes, it's the interviewer). One caveat: a state can hide a trait as well as mimic one, and a schizoid patient may call himself a party animal while manic. In practice you'll need few probes, because signal signs and symptoms will already have eliminated many regions and pointed to others. Probes can be used anywhere in the body of the interview, but personality pathology leaps out in the social history, where patients describe its effect on past relationships and jobs, and by then the alliance usually allows sensitive questions." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "Limiting the field in the social history", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "After college, what did you do for work?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Couple of months at a utility, then sales, then... a lot of jobs." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Over the next five years, how many would you say? Three? Ten? Twenty?", tag: "symptom amplification" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Close to twenty, probably." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Did any of those end because of trouble with the law or a boss asking you to leave? Sometimes that's why people move on fast.", tag: "probing antisocial, with shame attenuation" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Never been arrested. Police scare me. I don't even do drugs; it's not right." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "What do you do for fun?", tag: "antisocial unlikely; switch to the social region" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Not much. I'm kind of a loner." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "If you had more skill with people, would you want to be around them more?", tag: "schizoid or avoidant?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "I'd love to. But I always get hurt. And it makes me furious." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Those bosses you mentioned: did you get into arguments with them?", tag: "anger and firings argue against dependent and avoidant" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Fired twice. Fifty-fifty whose fault. When I get home I throw things. People treat me like dirt; that's why I'm always depressed." }
        ], note: "Composed, after the chapter's illustration. In a few minutes: antisocial unlikely (strong conscience, no legal history), schizoid ruled out (wants people), dependent and avoidant unlikely (anger, firings, job hopping), schizotypal out (no psychotic material earlier). Borderline is the best bet, histrionic less likely (few histrionic people see themselves as socially inept or grow reclusive), passive-aggressive traits noted, and a mixed picture is possible. Now expand borderline thoroughly and check the histrionic criteria." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c14p2", label: "Practice: using a probe to rule out" }, { kind: "drill", id: "personality-probes", label: "Drill: Personality probe questions" }]
    },

    {
      id: "expand",
      title: "Step 2: expanding a diagnosis, and Mrs. Jacobs",
      lede: "Expanding borderline criteria in ten minutes while staying engaged, anchoring to adolescence, and ruling out state dependency.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Having narrowed the field, you expand the likeliest diagnosis criterion by criterion. Two problems arise at once, the same two that limit any first-interview diagnosis: showing within the time available that the pattern has been present since adolescence, and making sure the reported behavior is valid. Validity is hardest with subjective criteria, because the patient and you may mean different things ('Are you overly perfectionistic?' 'Yes.' By whose standard?). You also need intimate familiarity with each disorder's DSM-5 criteria, from study and above all from experience, joined to a phenomenological feel for how this person is in the world. Shea uses borderline personality disorder as his example because many of its criteria are behavioral and the subjective ones are distinctive, so it can often be diagnosed in one interview; and because DBT and the other effective therapies can't be offered until someone spots it." },
        { type: "case", title: "Mrs. Jacobs", text: "Summarized from the chapter. Transferred from another hospital, where on a brief pass she had cut off her wristband, bought aspirin at the drugstore around the corner, swallowed all 24 tablets, and waited before casually telling the nurses. She spoke with a curious blandness, as if about someone else, with deliberately sparing eye contact; one previous major depression, no mania. In about ten minutes the interviewer heard: mixed feelings about the psychiatrist she felt was 'dumping' her (she had warned him; her husband was furious at him); a lifelong pull toward older guiding men, teachers and a minister she saw as almost a saint until she realized she was one of hundreds to him, and then he 'used her as a doormat'; no friendship lasting more than four or five years, ending in rows because people take advantage of her; anger mostly bottled and then explosive, a plate thrown at her husband on her last pass, his camera smashed, and, when she was her usual self rather than depressed, more broken things than she could count, the biggest being her husband's nose (both laughed); a fist through the wall in her school years; head banging long ago; then, rolling up her sleeve, wrist scars from cutting that didn't hurt and made 'it' feel over, which she would do again, and he couldn't stop her. Asked about mood: rapid shifts within a day since high school; when a friend leaves and the door shuts, feeling a piece of her torn away, sometimes anger, sometimes cutting; and not boredom but deadness, as if part of her weren't in the room. She worried about a locked ward, then coolly said they'd better lock her up. Mood instability, emptiness, idealization and devaluation, inappropriate anger, self-damaging acts, and intolerance of being alone: probably enough for the diagnosis, with good rapport (she said afterward she liked the interviewer)." },
        { type: "p", text: "Validity begins with engagement. Expansion must flow naturally, never as a checklist (Chapter 4), with room to sidetrack when the patient needs to; the joke about the husband's nose eased her and showed the clinician was listening, not filling a form. When she challenged him that she would cut again and he couldn't stop her, he didn't counter-attack or tell her not to; visible fear is exactly what invites a war of nerves. He agreed calmly that only she could stop it, set the topic aside, and went on, returning later to ask what she felt while cutting, what she felt when she stopped, and whether she had been trying to die. Her world is one of circular contradictions: the husband who protects her becomes 'such a mean person'; the saintly minister becomes a demon for not giving her all his attention. Battling a feeling of deadness, she attacks first and challenges him to a duel over who rules her wrists, which he wisely declines. The cool 'they'd better lock me up' right after his concern is what clinicians call being 'jerked around'; it is less manipulation than an inadequate, sometimes useful, defense against pain. See the frightened child and the adult, help them stop being enemies, and countertransference stays low and validity high." },
        { type: "steps", items: [
          "Verify historical persistence. Introduce questions with anchors such as 'From high school onward, have you found...' or 'Looking at your life from adolescence until now, is it typical for you to...', about five times or more in each expansion. In Mrs. Jacobs's interview: 'back during your school years, what were your friendships like?', 'back in school, did you ever punch a wall?', 'did your parents or high-school friends see you as moody?'",
          "Rule out [[State dependency]]. A behavior caused by mania, depression, psychosis, or substances cannot count toward a personality criterion. Name the time period explicitly ('I know you've been depressed recently; when you're feeling your normal self, how many times have you broken things in anger?'). Unless you keep making the period explicit, patients merge their episodes with their baseline and the history is badly distorted.",
          "Verify pathological severity (next section)."
        ] }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c14p1", label: "Practice: anchoring to adolescence" }, { kind: "practice", id: "c14p4", label: "Practice: staying non-defensive when provoked" }]
    },

    {
      id: "severity",
      title: "Verifying severity: tapping for epiphenomena",
      lede: "When a trait becomes a criterion, the generic and specific epiphenomena questions, and Tommy.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Behaviors are dimensional: a trait may be beneficial or damaging depending on its frequency, severity, and rigidity. Rigid attention to detail could paralyze an emergency nurse triaging after a disaster, yet you'd want an emergency nurse with better-than-average attention to detail, used flexibly, who stays in the room until she has the best read she can get on a patient's suicide risk rather than shrugging that it's a coin toss. Though the DSM-5 is categorical, it implicitly requires a dimensional judgment for every personality criterion: a trait meets a criterion only if it is severe and rigid enough to cause significant pain to the patient or others, and the DSM-5 warns against calling upper-normal traits pathological when the person functions well in their role. Not all actors are histrionic, though all must be comfortable at center stage; not all surgeons are narcissistic, though all need an unusual sense of being right." },
        { type: "p", text: "Checking this sounds slow and is not. Ask whether the trait is present ('Are you perfectionistic, maybe to the point of it being a problem?'). A laughing 'I wish; bosses call me sloppy' lets you move on. But a 'yes' proves little, since most trainees would say yes. If the trait is truly pathological, it will produce a cluster of associated experiences, epiphenomena. [[Tapping for epiphenomena]] uses a special kind of behavioral incident (Pascal's, Chapter 5): after a yes, ask a generic epiphenomena question such as 'What makes you say that?' or 'Can you give me an example?' People whose trait is pathological can usually rattle off an example or two of trouble it has caused them or others (the man who was still painting the library door while his wife finished three walls and the bookshelves). Watch the process as well as the content: a quick answer and a natural, recognizing expression suggest the trait is familiar to them. If it's still unclear, ask a specific epiphenomena question about a typical consequence: 'Do you have trouble finishing on time?' 'Has anyone complained that you take too long?'" },
        { type: "case", title: "Tommy", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A neat, meticulous college student, self-referred for anxiety about schoolwork; signal signs and symptoms had narrowed the field to obsessive–compulsive and avoidant personality. Entering through a referred gate (his excellent grades), Shea asked if he was perfectionistic; yes. 'How do you know?' He avoids tasks he fears he won't do right, and takes criticism badly. Lifelong? 'My whole life', said quickly and sincerely. A normalized question about not letting others do things for him brought a yes and, asked why, an embarrassed half-admission that others wouldn't do it as well, cut off because saying so felt rude. On breaks at work he tells himself he should be studying, an answer many people might give, so Shea asked a specific epiphenomena question: did he laugh a lot? 'Never', only the little laugh you give a joke on television. That sounded sad; yes, it was, and had been for a few years. In under three minutes three obsessive–compulsive criteria were met with severity and persistence (perfectionism, reluctance to delegate, overly serious devotion to work); five more minutes showed he met all but one." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "Tapping a 'yes'", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "You mentioned you check your work a lot. Would you call yourself a perfectionist?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Definitely." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "What makes you say that?", tag: "generic epiphenomena question" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "(quickly) My reports are always late because I redo them. My supervisor wrote it in my evaluation twice." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Has that been true since you were in school?", tag: "persistence" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "I used to stay up all night recopying notes so they'd be neat. My mom would find me at the table at four." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "And outside of work, does it ever get in the way at home?", tag: "specific epiphenomena question, other people affected" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "My wife says I make every chore take three times longer." }
        ], note: "Composed. The quick, concrete, slightly rueful examples, at work, in school, and at home, show a trait that is rigid, long-standing, and costly: it meets the criterion. A hesitant 'I guess I like things neat' would not." },
        { type: "p", text: "Shea's Video Module 14.1 shows this kind of expansion in real time for antisocial personality disorder, a diagnosis many clinicians find hard to pin down at intake, combining the Chapter 5 validity techniques with this chapter's strategy." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c14p3", label: "Practice: tapping for epiphenomena" }]
    },

    {
      id: "dimensions",
      title: "Dimensions, hidden strengths, and the future",
      lede: "The Alternative DSM-5 Model, entrance questions, Nancy's strength, prototype systems, and clinicians as story collectors.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The DSM-5 originally meant to replace the categorical personality system with a largely dimensional hybrid and instead placed it in an appendix as the [[Alternative DSM-5 Model for Personality Disorders]]. Dimensional systems paint a truer, more individual picture, which matters greatly for something as personal as personality, but the more traits they cover, the longer the interview. The alternative model rates 25 trait facets (emotional lability, submissiveness, intimacy avoidance, deceitfulness, impulsivity, and others). Shea thinks it would take a skilled interviewer at least an hour to do well, impractical for the 7 to 12 minutes an intake has, and the literature has raised many similar criticisms. The categorical system, unchanged from the DSM-IV, remains surprisingly good at producing a reasonable, if imperfect, differential in that time. Where there is time, as for a long-term therapist, the DSM-5 permits using the alternative model instead." },
        { type: "p", text: "Its trait facets are still valuable now: as an overview of traits that cause real trouble, as clear targets for collaborative treatment planning, and as entry points into a patient's world over the course of therapy. In an intake, as time allows, raise one or two traits that seem pressing, use behavioral incidents to judge severity, and use generic and specific epiphenomena questions to confirm that the trait is present, pathological, and valid. Shea offers [[Entrance question|entrance questions]] for each facet (read the full table in the book); they resemble the probes and lean heavily on historical anchors like 'from middle school on' or 'from your mid-teens to now' to show a true trait rather than a state." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Trait domain", "Facets", "A composed entrance question"], rows: [
          ["Negative affectivity", "Emotional lability, anxiousness, separation insecurity, perseveration, submissiveness, hostility, depressivity, suspiciousness", "Since your teens, have people often told you that you overreact, or to calm down when you felt things were fine?"],
          ["Detachment", "Restricted affectivity, withdrawal, anhedonia, intimacy avoidance (depressivity and suspiciousness also count here)", "If I asked people who know you, would they say you're easy or hard to get to know?"],
          ["Antagonism", "Manipulativeness, deceitfulness, grandiosity, attention seeking, callousness", "Over the years, has getting people to do what you want come easily to you?"],
          ["Disinhibition", "Irresponsibility, impulsivity, distractibility, risk taking, (lack of) rigid perfectionism", "Looking back from your teens to now, are you someone who plans things out, or someone who lets life happen?"],
          ["Psychoticism", "Unusual beliefs and experiences, eccentricity, cognitive and perceptual dysregulation", "Over the years, have people often found your ideas strange, or called you a bit eccentric?"]
        ] },
        { type: "case", title: "Nancy", text: "Summarized from the chapter. In a second session with a woman diagnosed with borderline personality disorder the week before (she had welcomed the diagnosis as finally explaining her behavior), the clinician explored impulsivity, since she regretted many life decisions. She laughed: she had never made an impulsive decision; she weighed every pro and con over and over, driving her spouse mad, and regretted decisions because she second-guessed them. A bit of a problem, she agreed, but nothing next to the screaming, mood swings, tantrums, and cutting. The clinician called it a hidden strength: most people he had seen with borderline personality disorder struggle badly with impulsive decisions, and she had found a system that works. Perhaps the same pro-and-con weighing could be turned on her angry outbursts before they happen. She perked up; she had never seen it that way. He moved on to risk taking ('I'm a scaredy cat'). Not everyone with a diagnosis shows all its usual traits; her compulsive streak checks her impulsivity, it can be used in therapy, and a person with chronic worthlessness now has something to be proud of. Healing has begun." },
        { type: "p", text: "For the future, Shea suggests building on the categorical system rather than replacing it: keep the criteria and add dimensional traits as optional specifiers, explored as time allows, just as specifiers are used for depression, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia, substance use, and PTSD. Over later sessions a more sculpted secondary description of the personality would emerge. Another route is [[Prototype-based system|prototype-based]] diagnosis, which drops individual criteria in favor of a narrative portrait; the patient is diagnosed if they largely match it. Shea favors Shedler and Westen's SWAP-II, which builds on the DSM categories; its portrait of the 'Borderline-Dysregulated Personality' (read it in the book) captures emotional dysregulation, unstable views of self and others, chaotic relationships, fear of abandonment, the pull it exerts on others to play unfamiliar roles, and self-destructive impulses. Criterion systems are strong on reliability; prototype systems are strong on validity, with high [[Descriptive essence]]." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Professional story collectors", text: "Shea closes with Neil Gaiman on the vital importance of stories. Our patients with severe personality disorders are storytellers whose stories are all too real: abuse, divorce, death, illness, depression, voices, cutting. Their behaviors, which none of them asked for, create chaos and rejection, and they affect clinicians too. We are not storytellers but professional story collectors, and collecting the whole, valid story is at the heart of healing. The intensity of rage, the thirst of dependency, and the depth of suicidal angst can tempt even well-meaning clinicians to turn the story off before it is heard. Chapter 15's psychodynamic lens is meant to help us keep listening." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c14p6", label: "Practice: finding a hidden strength" }, { kind: "quiz", id: "ch14", label: "Quiz: Chapter 14" }]
    }
  ],

  deeper: [
    { title: "Video Module 14.1", text: "Sensitively Uncovering Criteria for Antisocial Personality Disorder: expanded didactics and an annotated interview excerpt expanding the antisocial criteria in real time, using the Chapter 5 validity techniques with this chapter's two-step strategy." },
    { title: "Read in the book", text: "Shea's full portraits of the ten personality disorders; Figures 14.1 and 14.2 summarizing signal signs and signal symptoms; the complete probe-question lists for each disorder (several by Jeremy Roberts) with their keyed answers; the full transcripts of the limiting-the-field illustration, Mrs. Jacobs, Tommy, and Nancy; Table 14.1, entrance questions for all 25 trait facets of the Alternative DSM-5 Model; and the SWAP-II Borderline-Dysregulated prototype." },
    { title: "Chapters this one connects to", text: "Chapter 13 for the core concepts (historical diagnosis, social history, label theory, map and terrain); Chapter 15 for the psychodynamics (splitting, object relations, self psychology) that make these behaviors understandable; Chapter 3 for scouting and PACE; Chapter 4 for natural and referred gates and conversational expansion; Chapter 5 for behavioral incidents, shame attenuation, symptom amplification, and normalization; Chapter 8 for Ms. Dole and parent–child nonverbal reciprocals; Chapter 9 for the manic episode that label slapping misses; Chapter 11 for micropsychotic episodes." },
    { title: "Sources Shea draws on", text: "The DSM-5 and its Alternative Model for Personality Disorders; Shedler and Westen's SWAP-II and their work on prototype diagnosis; Vaillant on the pain beneath antisocial behavior; Jeremy Roberts's probe questions; Pascal's behavioral incident; Linehan's DBT; Carl Rogers on unconditional positive regard; and the many critiques of the Alternative Model's practicality." }
  ]
};
